#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179597 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179735 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "5d7c30c2-5d42-5648-ae49-5a0c7ec43233" :knowledge-point/title "Line Integrals Over Circles and Ellipses"}] :question/id #uuid "e4893301-34d3-5f63-9d1c-715588d98d40" :question/math-academy-id "e-9531" :question/problem "Evaluate $\\displaystyle \\oint\\limits_{C} \\mathbf{F}(\\mathbf{r}) \\cdot \\textrm{d}\\mathbf{r},$ where $\\mathbf F(x, y) = y\\,\\mathbf{i} + x\\,\\mathbf{j}$ and $C$ is a circle of radius $1$ centered at the origin, traversed once in the counterclockwise direction." :question/worked-solution "The notation $\\displaystyle\\oint$ is used to remind us that we're dealing with a line integral over a *closed* curve.\n\nWe will use the formula\n\n$$\n\\oint\\limits_C \\mathbf{F} \\cdot \\textrm{d}\\mathbf{r} = \\int_a^b \\mathbf{F}(\\mathbf{r}(t)) \\cdot \\mathbf{r}'(t) \\,\\textrm{d}t.\n$$\n\nFirst, we need to parameterize a circle of radius $1$ centered at the origin. It can be parameterized as \n\n$$\n\\mathbf{r}(t) = \\cos{t}\\,\\mathbf{i} + \\sin{t}\\,\\mathbf{j},\n\\qquad 0 \\leq t \\lt 2\\pi.\n$$\n\nSo, along the curve $C,$ we have\n\n$$\nx = \\cos{t}, \\qquad\ny = \\sin{t}.\n$$\n\nAs a result,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(t)) & =y\\,i+x\\,j=\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,i+\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing $\\mathbf{r}'(t),$ we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)j \\\\  & =-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,i+\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t) & =(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,i+\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,j)\\cdot (-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,i+\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,j) \\\\  & =-{sin}^{2}⁡t+{cos}^{2}⁡t \\\\  & =-(1-{cos}^{2}⁡t)+{cos}^{2}⁡t \\\\  & =2{cos}^{2}⁡t-1 \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can evaluate the integral, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}F(r)\\cdot \\text{d}r & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }F(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t\\,dt \\\\  & ={[\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t]}_{0}^{2\\pi } \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡4\\pi -\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(0) \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179598 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179736 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "f6eddefb-d4b9-5e5b-9278-94de0d8dbff6" :knowledge-point/title "Line Integrals Over Cartesian Curves"}] :question/id #uuid "a448227d-9bf3-54dd-8639-cbdc56a5a403" :question/math-academy-id "e-9532" :question/problem "Evaluate $\\displaystyle \\int\\limits_{C} \\mathbf{F} \\cdot \\textrm{d}\\mathbf{r},$ where $\\mathbf F(x, y) = y\\,\\mathbf{i} + x\\,\\mathbf{j}$ and $C$ is the curve $y^3 = x-5$ traversed from the point $A(5,0)$ to the point $B(6,1).$" :question/worked-solution "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n\\int\\limits_C \\mathbf{F} \\cdot \\textrm{d}\\mathbf{r} = \\int_a^b \\mathbf{F}(\\mathbf{r}(t)) \\cdot \\mathbf{r}'(t) \\,\\textrm{d}t.\n$$\n\nFirst, we need to parameterize the curve. Making $x$ the subject of the equation, we have\n\n$$\ny^3 = x-5\n\\qquad \\Longrightarrow \\qquad\nx = y^3 + 5.\n$$\n\nSo, we can parametrize the curve using\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =({t}^{3}+5)\\,i+t\\,j,\\,0\\le t\\le 1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, along the curve $C,$ we have\n\n$$\nx = t^3+5, \\qquad\ny = t.\n$$\n\nAs a result, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(t)) & =y\\,i+x\\,j \\\\  & =t\\,i+({t}^{3}+5)\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing $\\mathbf{r}'(t),$ we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{3}+5)i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t)j \\\\  & =3{t}^{2}\\,i+j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t) & =(t\\,i+({t}^{3}+5)\\,j)\\cdot (3{t}^{2}\\,i+j) \\\\  & =t\\cdot 3{t}^{2}+({t}^{3}+5)\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =3{t}^{3}+{t}^{3}+5 \\\\  & =4{t}^{3}+5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate the integral, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}F(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}4{t}^{3}+5\\,dt \\\\  & ={[{t}^{4}+5t]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =1+5-0 \\\\  & =6\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179599 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179737 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "0983e1e6-46a9-53de-83f5-a28d5b1eee47" :knowledge-point/title "Line Integrals Over Line Segments"}] :question/id #uuid "9152629f-47a8-5a71-9bd9-3f5205788f5d" :question/math-academy-id "e-9533" :question/problem "Evaluate $\\displaystyle \\int\\limits_{C} \\mathbf{F} \\cdot \\textrm{d}\\mathbf{r},$ where $\\mathbf F(x, y) =(1-y)^2\\,\\mathbf{i} + x\\,\\mathbf{j}$ and $C$ is the line segment traversed from the point $A(0, 2)$ to the point $B(1,0).$" :question/worked-solution "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n\\int\\limits_C \\mathbf{F} \\cdot \\textrm{d}\\mathbf{r} = \\int_a^b \\mathbf{F}(\\mathbf{r}(t)) \\cdot \\mathbf{r}'(t) \\,\\textrm{d}t.\n$$\n\nFirst, we need to parameterize the line segment.\nThe position vectors of the endpoints of our line segment are \n$\\mathbf{a} = 2\\mathbf{j}$ and $\\mathbf{b} = \\mathbf{i}.$\nSo, the parametrization of the line segment is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =a+t(b-a) \\\\  & =2j+t[i-2j] \\\\  & =t\\,i+(2-2t)\\,j,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $t$ varies from $t=0$ (at the point $A$) to $t = 1$ (at the point $B$).\nThus, along the curve $C,$ we have\n\n$$\nx = t, \\qquad\n\ty = 2-2t.\n$$\n\nAs a result, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(t)) & =(1-y)^{2}\\,i+x\\,j \\\\  & =(1-(2-2t))^{2}\\,i+t\\,j \\\\  & =(2t-1)^{2}\\,i+t\\,j \\\\  & =(4{t}^{2}-4t+1)\\,i+t\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing $\\mathbf{r}'(t),$ we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t)i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2-2t)j \\\\  & =i-2j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t) & =((4{t}^{2}-4t+1)\\,i+t\\,j)\\cdot (i-2j) \\\\  & =(4{t}^{2}-4t+1)\\cdot (1)+(t)\\cdot (-2) \\\\  & =4{t}^{2}-6t+1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate the integral, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}F(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}4{t}^{2}-6t+1\\,dt \\\\  & =(\\frac{4}{3}{t}^{3}-3{t}^{2}+t){|}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =(\\frac{4}{3}-3+1)-0 \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180012 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179736 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "f6eddefb-d4b9-5e5b-9278-94de0d8dbff6" :knowledge-point/title "Line Integrals Over Cartesian Curves"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1f27ef8e-0147-52c1-a878-3bfc73978cca" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{1}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+4t\\operatorname{cos}⁡({t}^{2})\\,dt" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186414839} {:answer/id #uuid "4b526b24-81f5-50f4-b735-c24b3e77e386" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{1}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+4t\\operatorname{sin}⁡({t}^{2}-1)\\,dt" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186414849} {:answer/id #uuid "6a0e0ea2-e6aa-590e-84ad-cadfa81c2549" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{1}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+4t\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2{t}^{2}-1)\\,dt" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186414858} {:answer/id #uuid "7cd06a3d-39cb-5a6e-a180-c17cff148c28" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{1}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+4t\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2{t}^{2}-1)\\,dt" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186414865} {:answer/id #uuid "8fbf77d8-aea4-5c04-bc8a-1b07a1866351" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{1}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+4t\\operatorname{sin}⁡({t}^{2})\\,dt" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186414877}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7cd06a3d-39cb-5a6e-a180-c17cff148c28" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{1}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+4t\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2{t}^{2}-1)\\,dt" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186414865} :answer-field/id #uuid "243882e8-5b20-5cd8-9637-d6f35b444dae" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186414904}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "d4e0663c-1824-56f9-b923-60eb7cd3a338" :question/math-academy-id "q-124703" :question/problem "Which of the following is equivalent to the integral ${\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r,$ where $F(x,y)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,i+\\operatorname{sin}⁡y\\,j$ and $C$ is the curve $y=2{x}^{2}-1$ traversed from the point $A(1,1)$ to the point $B(2,7)?$\n\n*Hint: Use the parametrization $r(t)=t\\,i+(2{t}^{2}-1)\\,j.$*" :question/worked-solution "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r={\\int }_{a}^{b}F(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t)\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nFirst, we need to parameterize the curve. This is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =t\\,i+(2{t}^{2}-1)\\,j,\\,1\\le t\\le 2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, along the curve $C,$ we have\n\n$$\nx=t,\\,y=2{t}^{2}-1.\n$$\n\nAs a result, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(t)) & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,i+\\operatorname{sin}⁡y\\,j \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,i+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2{t}^{2}-1)\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing ${r}^{′}(t),$ we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t)i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2{t}^{2}-1)j \\\\  & =i+4t\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t) & =(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,i+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2{t}^{2}-1)\\,j)\\cdot (i+4t\\,j) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\cdot 1+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2{t}^{2}-1)\\cdot 4t \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+4t\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2{t}^{2}-1).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can write the integral as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}F(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+4t\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2{t}^{2}-1)\\,dt.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180015 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179735 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "5d7c30c2-5d42-5648-ae49-5a0c7ec43233" :knowledge-point/title "Line Integrals Over Circles and Ellipses"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "09128715-0b6a-5c9a-b7c4-82cfe1932e05" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186414832} {:answer/id #uuid "5f6a55ec-5d2e-5ff0-b433-60144af0675f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186414854} {:answer/id #uuid "64f18f1c-5e0b-5164-a6fc-70267c5edda2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186414857} {:answer/id #uuid "76c57faf-8ecf-5560-9acf-a26154142b47" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186414862} {:answer/id #uuid "c895dd03-f0b4-5188-9445-aa181d036f50" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186414891}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c895dd03-f0b4-5188-9445-aa181d036f50" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186414891} :answer-field/id #uuid "84019ffa-a424-5feb-9be8-03a303ad719a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186414910}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "2a8909b1-1a48-5c56-ac06-8b954196fc3e" :question/math-academy-id "q-124936" :question/problem "Evaluate ${∮}_{C}F(r)\\cdot \\text{d}r,$ where $F(x,y)=y\\,i-x\\,j$ and $C$ is a circle of radius $1$ centered at the origin, traversed once in the counterclockwise direction." :question/worked-solution "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r={\\int }_{a}^{b}F(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t)\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nFirst, we need to parameterize a circle of radius $1$ centered at the origin. It can be parameterized as \n\n$$\nr(t)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,i+\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,j,\\,0\\le t\\le 2\\pi .\n$$\n\nSo, along the curve $C,$ we have\n\n$$\nx=\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,y=\\operatorname{sin}⁡t.\n$$\n\nAs a result,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(t)) & =y\\,i-x\\,j \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,i-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing ${r}^{′}(t),$ we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)j \\\\  & =-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,i+\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t) & =(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,i-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,j)\\cdot (-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,i+\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,j) \\\\  & =-{sin}^{2}⁡t-{cos}^{2}⁡t \\\\  & =-({sin}^{2}⁡t+{cos}^{2}⁡t) \\\\  & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can evaluate the integral, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}F(r)\\cdot \\text{d}r & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }F(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }(-1)\\,dt \\\\  & ={-t|}_{0}^{2\\pi } \\\\  & =-2\\pi \\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186414924 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179736 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "f6eddefb-d4b9-5e5b-9278-94de0d8dbff6" :knowledge-point/title "Line Integrals Over Cartesian Curves"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0b0df5d3-d93a-5f24-a344-46796aad3425" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "16" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186414834} {:answer/id #uuid "7d942498-56d2-5693-bfad-80844fd7484a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186414866} {:answer/id #uuid "ab090754-b66d-50d4-b567-d44fb10bf4dc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186414883} {:answer/id #uuid "b194be39-9f92-58e1-8d86-d43308338769" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186414885} {:answer/id #uuid "c53f8b68-f2cc-5a88-83ab-422d342a00aa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "25" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186414888}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0b0df5d3-d93a-5f24-a344-46796aad3425" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "16" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186414834} :answer-field/id #uuid "53f24906-9587-5d21-abdc-d9f2ac613180" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186414908}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "78546b3f-b852-5bf0-8988-10eb32837ae5" :question/math-academy-id "q-125028" :question/problem "Evaluate ${\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r,$ where $F(x,y)=x\\,i+y\\,j$ and $C$ is the curve $y=\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+9}$ traversed from the point $A(0,3)$ to the point $B(4,5).$" :question/worked-solution "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r={\\int }_{a}^{b}F(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t)\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nFirst, we need to parameterize the curve. Since $y$ is the subject of the equation, a parametrization is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =t\\,i+\\sqrt{{t}^{2}+9}\\,j,\\,0\\le t\\le 4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, along the curve $C,$ we have\n\n$$\nx=t,\\,y=\\sqrt{{t}^{2}+9}.\n$$\n\nAs a result, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(t)) & =t\\,i+\\sqrt{{t}^{2}+9}\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing ${r}^{′}(t),$ we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t)i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\sqrt{{t}^{2}+9})j \\\\  & =i+\\frac{t}{\\sqrt{{t}^{2}+9}}\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t) & =(t\\,i+\\sqrt{{t}^{2}+9}\\,j)\\cdot (i+\\frac{t}{\\sqrt{{t}^{2}+9}}\\,j) \\\\  & =t\\cdot 1+\\sqrt{{t}^{2}+9}\\cdot \\frac{t}{\\sqrt{{t}^{2}+9}} \\\\  & =2t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate the integral, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r & ={\\int }_{0}^{4}F(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{4}2t\\,dt \\\\  & ={t}^{2}{|}_{0}^{4} \\\\  & =16\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186414925 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179736 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "f6eddefb-d4b9-5e5b-9278-94de0d8dbff6" :knowledge-point/title "Line Integrals Over Cartesian Curves"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0b4c3f95-dd3c-596d-a9e6-8060319b1532" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186414835} {:answer/id #uuid "40470b51-3b88-5d4f-8bd4-6247bc36d0d5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2e" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186414847} {:answer/id #uuid "9dfdf355-e005-5cd0-87b3-f31a190f0393" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186414879} {:answer/id #uuid "b38bdbbe-9a36-505e-83dd-b13a689c1caf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2{e}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186414886} {:answer/id #uuid "c725c714-e481-5064-9de4-255e575a48ff" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4e" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186414889}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b38bdbbe-9a36-505e-83dd-b13a689c1caf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2{e}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186414886} :answer-field/id #uuid "ce53d1c1-5bca-58e1-b9ee-b33918bcfde9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186414915}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "88b1af01-ca8c-54ff-810f-7581fa6aee53" :question/math-academy-id "q-125036" :question/problem "Evaluate ${\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r,$ where $F(x,y)={e}^{x}\\,i+\\operatorname{ln}⁡y\\,j$ and $C$ is the curve $y={e}^{x}$ traversed from the point $A(0,1)$ to the point $B(2,{e}^{2}).$" :question/worked-solution "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r={\\int }_{a}^{b}F(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t)\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nFirst, we need to parameterize the curve. Since $y$ is the subject of the equation, a parametrization is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =t\\,i+{e}^{t}\\,j,\\,0\\le t\\le 2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, along the curve $C,$ we have\n\n$$\nx=t,\\,y={e}^{t}.\n$$\n\nAs a result, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(t)) & ={e}^{t}\\,i+\\operatorname{ln}⁡({e}^{t})\\,j \\\\  & ={e}^{t}\\,i+t\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing ${r}^{′}(t),$ we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t)i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({e}^{t})j \\\\  & =i+{e}^{t}\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t) & =({e}^{t}\\,i+t\\,j)\\cdot (i+{e}^{t}\\,j) \\\\  & ={e}^{t}+t{e}^{t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate the integral, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}F(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}({e}^{t}+t{e}^{t})\\,dt \\\\  & =t{e}^{t}{|}_{0}^{2} \\\\  & =2{e}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186414921 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179737 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "0983e1e6-46a9-53de-83f5-a28d5b1eee47" :knowledge-point/title "Line Integrals Over Line Segments"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "86348114-e8cd-5a6d-a76f-11ef3ab59583" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{1}\\sqrt[3]{2t-1}+(t+1)^{6}\\,dt" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186414873} {:answer/id #uuid "8a361507-da7c-560a-aee3-70e11ae1d221" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{1}\\sqrt[3]{t+1}+2(2t-1)^{6}\\,dt" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186414875} {:answer/id #uuid "a1324a16-4fd0-50ed-80fb-ce571c8b7bba" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{1}2\\sqrt[3]{2t-1}+(t+1)^{6}\\,dt" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186414880} {:answer/id #uuid "a2d5d8b9-aae7-5743-8533-8a9cb8d416d1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{1}\\sqrt[3]{t+1}+(2t-1)^{6}\\,dt" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186414881} {:answer/id #uuid "d829f57e-6fae-5c40-9e4d-5539c48d2670" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{1}\\sqrt[3]{2t}+{t}^{6}\\,dt" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186414895}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a1324a16-4fd0-50ed-80fb-ce571c8b7bba" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{1}2\\sqrt[3]{2t-1}+(t+1)^{6}\\,dt" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186414880} :answer-field/id #uuid "479e7987-52fe-5f2a-ac6f-211f33047ce8" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186414906}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "539feec3-1822-501c-b96b-4b9de8ad853a" :question/math-academy-id "q-125042" :question/problem "Which of the following is equivalent to ${\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r,$ where $F(x,y)=\\sqrt[3]{x}\\,i+{y}^{6}\\,j$ and $C$ is the line segment traversed from the point $A(-1,1)$ to the point $B(1,2)?$\n\n*Hint: Use the parametrization $r(t)=(-1+2t)\\,i+(1+t)\\,j.$*" :question/worked-solution "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r={\\int }_{a}^{b}F(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t)\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nFirst, we need to parameterize the line segment.\nThe position vectors of the endpoints of our line segment are \n$a=-\\,i+\\,j$ and $b=i+2\\,j.$\nSo, the parametrization of the line segment is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =a+t(b-a) \\\\  & =(-i+\\,j)+t[(i+2\\,j)-(-i+j)] \\\\  & =(-i+\\,j)+t(2\\,i+\\,j) \\\\  & =(-1+2t)\\,i+(1+t)\\,j,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $t$ varies from $t=0$ (at the point $A$) to $t=1$ (at the point $B$).\nThus, along the curve $C,$ we have\n\n$$\nx=-1+2t,\\,y=1+t.\n$$\n\nAs a result, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(t)) & =\\sqrt[3]{x}\\,i+{y}^{6}\\,j \\\\  & =\\sqrt[3]{-1+2t}\\,i+(1+t)^{6}\\,j \\\\  & =\\sqrt[3]{2t-1}\\,i+(t+1)^{6}\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing ${r}^{′}(t),$ we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(-1+2t)i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(1+t)j \\\\  & =2\\,i+j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t) & =(\\sqrt[3]{2t-1}\\,i+(t+1)^{6}\\,j)\\cdot (2\\,i+j) \\\\  & =\\sqrt[3]{2t-1}\\cdot 2+(t+1)^{6}\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =2\\sqrt[3]{2t-1}+(t+1)^{6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can write the integral as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}F(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}2\\sqrt[3]{2t-1}+(t+1)^{6}\\,dt.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186414922 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179737 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "0983e1e6-46a9-53de-83f5-a28d5b1eee47" :knowledge-point/title "Line Integrals Over Line Segments"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "23d81859-b818-5dab-8b5b-0a4ad9b37a4c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{1}(t-1)^{2}+{e}^{5t-3}\\,dt" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186414841} {:answer/id #uuid "7e023ba3-cbaf-5bdd-8624-aebf72479eb9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{1}(t-3)^{2}+{e}^{t-1}\\,dt" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186414867} {:answer/id #uuid "81fc43fd-7ab5-5169-91d7-e110be9b07b7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{1}3{t}^{2}-2{e}^{t}\\,dt" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186414870} {:answer/id #uuid "8341fafe-480b-544d-a970-50af229d306a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{1}3(t-1)^{2}-2{e}^{5t-3}\\,dt" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186414871} {:answer/id #uuid "c83e8152-70e7-5e4f-8560-989d3d076b5f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{1}2(t-3)^{2}-3{e}^{5t-1}\\,dt" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186414890}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8341fafe-480b-544d-a970-50af229d306a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{1}3(t-1)^{2}-2{e}^{5t-3}\\,dt" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186414871} :answer-field/id #uuid "1f913e6a-566c-57ec-ae58-510ebea86214" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186414902}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "6205682e-6a34-5394-bdf6-22819f2b4d42" :question/math-academy-id "q-125044" :question/problem "Which of the following is equivalent to ${\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r,$ where $F(x,y)=(x+y)^{2}\\,i+{e}^{x-y}\\,j$ and $C$ is the line segment traversed from the point $A(-2,1)$ to the point $B(1,-1)?$\n\n*Hint: Use the parametrization $r(t)=(-2+3t)\\,i+(1-2t)\\,j.$*" :question/worked-solution "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r={\\int }_{a}^{b}F(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t)\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nFirst, we need to parameterize the line segment.\nThe position vectors of the endpoints of our line segment are \n$a=-2\\,i+j$ and $b=i-j.$\nSo, the parametrization of the line segment is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =a+t(b-a) \\\\  & =(-2\\,i+j)+t[(i-j)-(-2\\,i+j)] \\\\  & =(-2\\,i+j)+t(3\\,i-2\\,j) \\\\  & =(-2+3t)\\,i+(1-2t)\\,j,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $t$ varies from $t=0$ (at the point $A$) to $t=1$ (at the point $B$).\nThus, along the curve $C,$ we have\n\n$$\nx=-2+3t,\\,y=1-2t.\n$$\n\nAs a result, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(t)) & =(x+y)^{2}\\,i+{e}^{x-y}\\,j \\\\  & =(-2+3t+1-2t)^{2}\\,i+{e}^{-2+3t-(1-2t)}\\,j \\\\  & =(t-1)^{2}\\,i+{e}^{5t-3}\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing ${r}^{′}(t),$ we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(-2+3t)i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(1-2t)j \\\\  & =3\\,i-2\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t) & =((t-1)^{2}\\,i+{e}^{5t-2}\\,j)\\cdot (3\\,i-2\\,j) \\\\  & =(t-1)^{2}\\cdot 3+{e}^{5t-3}\\cdot (-2) \\\\  & =3(t-1)^{2}-2{e}^{5t-3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can write the integral as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}F(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}3(t-1)^{2}-2{e}^{5t-3}\\,dt.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186414928 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179737 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "0983e1e6-46a9-53de-83f5-a28d5b1eee47" :knowledge-point/title "Line Integrals Over Line Segments"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "23f5540a-2dfa-5b7f-a382-dd3019279e8a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{1}4\\operatorname{cos}⁡(1-2t)+6\\operatorname{sin}⁡(10t-3)\\,dt" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186414842} {:answer/id #uuid "30dc886f-658c-51b4-9f49-b8943b656fbb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{1}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(1-2t)+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(10t-3)\\,dt" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186414844} {:answer/id #uuid "6b236861-c420-5962-95a9-1206175b6e7e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{1}4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(-2t)+6\\operatorname{cos}⁡(10t)\\,dt" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186414860} {:answer/id #uuid "b0421b1d-fbb5-5870-919b-ff5926c3bc4f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{1}4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(1-2t)+6\\operatorname{cos}⁡(10t-3)\\,dt" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186414884} {:answer/id #uuid "d3b4a782-6c89-59a5-aed5-8248a2d18268" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{1}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(1-2t)+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(10t-3)\\,dt" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186414893}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "23f5540a-2dfa-5b7f-a382-dd3019279e8a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{1}4\\operatorname{cos}⁡(1-2t)+6\\operatorname{sin}⁡(10t-3)\\,dt" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186414842} :answer-field/id #uuid "cad5fa44-4c82-5eaf-a5e3-0f5d2ccc6820" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186414913}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "f6d2f16c-f6f5-5918-ae9d-9d7f59f7eba9" :question/math-academy-id "q-125045" :question/problem "Which of the following is equivalent to ${\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r,$ where $F(x,y)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x-y)\\,i+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x+y)\\,j$ and $C$ is the line segment traversed from the point $A(-1,-2)$ to the point $B(3,4)?$\n\n*Hint: Use the parametrization $r(t)=(-1+4t)\\,i+(-2+6t)\\,j.$*" :question/worked-solution "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r={\\int }_{a}^{b}F(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t)\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nFirst, we need to parameterize the line segment.\nThe position vectors of the endpoints of our line segment are \n$a=-i-2\\,j$ and $b=3\\,i+4\\,j.$\nSo, the parametrization of the line segment is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =a+t(b-a) \\\\  & =(-i-2\\,j)+t[(3\\,i+4\\,j)-(-i-2\\,j)] \\\\  & =(-i-2\\,j)+t(4\\,i+6\\,j) \\\\  & =(-1+4t)\\,i+(-2+6t)\\,j,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $t$ varies from $t=0$ (at the point $A$) to $t=1$ (at the point $B$).\nThus, along the curve $C,$ we have\n\n$$\nx=-1+4t,\\,y=-2+6t.\n$$\n\nAs a result, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(t)) & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x-y)\\,i+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x+y)\\,j \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡(-1+4t+2-6t)\\,i+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(-1+4t-2+6t)\\,j \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡(1-2t)\\,i+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(10t-3)\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing ${r}^{′}(t),$ we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(-1+4t)i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(-2+6t)j \\\\  & =4\\,i+6\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t) & =(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(1-2t)\\,i+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(10t-3)\\,j)\\cdot (4\\,i+6\\,j) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡(1-2t)\\cdot 4+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(10t-3)\\cdot 6 \\\\  & =4\\operatorname{cos}⁡(1-2t)+6\\operatorname{sin}⁡(10t-3).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can write the integral as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}F(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}4\\operatorname{cos}⁡(1-2t)+6\\operatorname{sin}⁡(10t-3)\\,dt.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186414920 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179737 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "0983e1e6-46a9-53de-83f5-a28d5b1eee47" :knowledge-point/title "Line Integrals Over Line Segments"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "36bd05a4-5096-56bb-a240-1215a5ed1dfd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186414846} {:answer/id #uuid "6adb2771-a945-5888-953b-e7abd4a45a34" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186414859} {:answer/id #uuid "7ea7328a-632b-5000-88e8-a0d8e9c325a3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186414868} {:answer/id #uuid "80816a28-9dc5-5444-944b-96509ad8006c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186414869} {:answer/id #uuid "dec0c058-ac21-5bc5-bc58-23ff911b63d4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186414897}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "80816a28-9dc5-5444-944b-96509ad8006c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186414869} :answer-field/id #uuid "22aada0e-297e-544e-8d81-c05bfd8f1190" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186414903}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "421f01b5-2cf6-557c-be0b-ca7da3ee40a5" :question/math-academy-id "q-125051" :question/problem "Evaluate ${\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r,$ where $F(x,y)=(y-x)^{2}\\,i-xy\\,j$ and $C$ is the line segment traversed from the point $A(-1,-1)$ to the point $B(-2,0).$" :question/worked-solution "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r={\\int }_{a}^{b}F(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t)\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nFirst, we need to parameterize the line segment.\nThe position vectors of the endpoints of our line segment are \n$a=-i-j$ and $b=-2\\,i.$\nSo, the parametrization of the line segment is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =a+t(b-a) \\\\  & =(-i-j)+t[-2\\,i-(-i-j)] \\\\  & =(-i-j)+t(-i+j) \\\\  & =(-1-t)\\,i+(t-1)\\,j,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $t$ varies from $t=0$ (at the point $A$) to $t=1$ (at the point $B$).\nThus, along the curve $C,$ we have\n\n$$\nx=-1-t,\\,y=t-1.\n$$\n\nAs a result, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(t)) & =(y-x)^{2}\\,i-xy\\,j \\\\  & =(t-1+t+1)^{2}\\,i-(t-1)(-1-t)\\,j \\\\  & =4{t}^{2}\\,i+({t}^{2}-1)\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing ${r}^{′}(t),$ we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(-1-t)i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t-1)j \\\\  & =-i+j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t) & =(4{t}^{2}\\,i+({t}^{2}-1)\\,j)\\cdot (-i+j) \\\\  & =4{t}^{2}\\cdot (-1)+({t}^{2}-1)\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =-3{t}^{2}-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate the integral, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}F(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}-3{t}^{2}-1\\,dt \\\\  & =(-{t}^{3}-t){|}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =(-1-1)-(-0-0) \\\\  & =-2\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186414919 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179737 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "0983e1e6-46a9-53de-83f5-a28d5b1eee47" :knowledge-point/title "Line Integrals Over Line Segments"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "223c9332-33d1-56cf-a05c-ba7cb8a837f9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186414840} {:answer/id #uuid "247b0b88-2614-5a47-9cda-0dcfe29a4e90" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186414843} {:answer/id #uuid "533b8ca1-8b6c-538a-ba14-32c3f6489f5d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186414850} {:answer/id #uuid "6c43b386-7e90-5fbc-bf2b-fc2a9db3e3e1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186414861} {:answer/id #uuid "8dac8507-5352-5564-8464-1744f7db369c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186414876}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "223c9332-33d1-56cf-a05c-ba7cb8a837f9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186414840} :answer-field/id #uuid "f6500bd7-63b7-5ff0-8a52-5b1a962545e2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186414916}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "32efcb37-d58e-5a20-a421-a7a716960b49" :question/math-academy-id "q-125055" :question/problem "Evaluate ${\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r,$ where $F(x,y)=4(x+y)\\,i-3x\\,j$ and $C$ is the line segment traversed from the point $A(2,1)$ to the point $B(3,3).$" :question/worked-solution "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r={\\int }_{a}^{b}F(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t)\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nFirst, we need to parameterize the line segment.\nThe position vectors of the endpoints of our line segment are \n$a=2\\,i+j$ and $b=3\\,i+3\\,j.$\nSo, the parametrization of the line segment is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =a+t(b-a) \\\\  & =(2\\,i+j)+t[(3\\,i+3\\,j)-(2\\,i+j)] \\\\  & =(2\\,i+j)+t(i+2j) \\\\  & =(t+2)\\,i+(2t+1)\\,j,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $t$ varies from $t=0$ (at the point $A$) to $t=1$ (at the point $B$).\nThus, along the curve $C,$ we have\n\n$$\nx=t+2\\,y=2t+1.\n$$\n\nAs a result, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(t)) & =4(x+y)\\,i-3x\\,j \\\\  & =4(3t+3)\\,i-(3t+6)\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing ${r}^{′}(t),$ we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t+2)i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2t+1)j \\\\  & =i+2\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t) & =(4(3t+3)\\,i-(3t+6)\\,j)\\cdot (i+2\\,j) \\\\  & =4(3t+3)\\cdot 1-(3t+6)\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =6t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate the integral, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}F(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}6t\\,dt \\\\  & =3{t}^{2}{|}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =3-0 \\\\  & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186414927 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179736 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "f6eddefb-d4b9-5e5b-9278-94de0d8dbff6" :knowledge-point/title "Line Integrals Over Cartesian Curves"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "02315312-3684-5faa-a71f-6939225f794e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{1}t(t+4{e}^{2t})\\,dt" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186414828} {:answer/id #uuid "775c713e-b491-5879-ae04-90cb3115c486" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{1}{t}^{2}(1+2{e}^{2t})\\,dt" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186414863} {:answer/id #uuid "d174d4e7-af1d-58cf-bd63-b6b4febdb84a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{1}{t}^{2}+2{e}^{2t}\\,dt" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186414892} {:answer/id #uuid "da2b15aa-cb0c-58bb-a375-02709c3833e3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{1}t(t+4)\\,dt" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186414896} {:answer/id #uuid "e445ee3a-80b3-5bb3-ba14-012400aa2c7c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{1}4t(1+{e}^{2t})\\,dt" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186414898}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "02315312-3684-5faa-a71f-6939225f794e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{1}t(t+4{e}^{2t})\\,dt" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186414828} :answer-field/id #uuid "41f1f87b-e755-58bb-9c62-b95f8a22f0a6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186414905}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "e11373c8-2017-5040-89cd-e12dc0c5ae7b" :question/math-academy-id "q-125115" :question/problem "Which of the following is equivalent to the integral ${\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r,$ where $F(x,y)={x}^{2}\\,i+\\operatorname{ln}⁡y\\,j$ and $C$ is the curve $y={e}^{2x}$ traversed from the point $A(0,1)$ to the point $B(1,{e}^{2})?$\n\n*Hint: Use the parametrization $r(t)=t\\,i+{e}^{2t}\\,j.$*" :question/worked-solution "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r={\\int }_{a}^{b}F(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t)\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nFirst, we need to parameterize the curve. This is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =t\\,i+{e}^{2t}\\,j,\\,0\\le t\\le 1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, along the curve $C,$ we have\n\n$$\nx=t,\\,y={e}^{2t}.\n$$\n\nAs a result, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(t)) & ={x}^{2}\\,i+\\operatorname{ln}⁡y\\,j \\\\  & ={t}^{2}\\,i+\\operatorname{ln}⁡{e}^{2t}\\,j \\\\  & ={t}^{2}\\,i+2t\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing ${r}^{′}(t),$ we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t)i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({e}^{2t})j \\\\  & =i+2{e}^{2t}\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t) & =({t}^{2}\\,i+2t\\,j)\\cdot (i+2{e}^{2t}\\,j) \\\\  & ={t}^{2}\\cdot 1+2t\\cdot 2{e}^{2t} \\\\  & ={t}^{2}+4t{e}^{2t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can write the integral as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}F(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{t}^{2}+4t{e}^{2t}\\,dt \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}t(t+4{e}^{2t})\\,dt.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186414923 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179736 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "f6eddefb-d4b9-5e5b-9278-94de0d8dbff6" :knowledge-point/title "Line Integrals Over Cartesian Curves"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "04de95bf-b731-52c7-8b53-b252f4ea7fbe" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{1}^{{e}^{2}}(\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{t}}+et)\\,dt" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186414830} {:answer/id #uuid "0e6cbea4-6185-5837-b0fb-bf0cbd81d253" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{1}^{e}(\\frac{{e}^{2}}{\\sqrt{t}}+t)\\,dt" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186414836} {:answer/id #uuid "316a1f2d-5fe6-5702-992f-674f470a0d8b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{1}^{{e}^{2}}(\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{t}}+{e}^{2}t)\\,dt" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186414845} {:answer/id #uuid "a6ccb2f3-c601-50dd-bdf2-b52ff269c8eb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{1}^{{e}^{2}}(\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{t}}+t)\\,dt" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186414882} {:answer/id #uuid "ee7cb40f-5e15-5d14-8beb-a1e42f226c9d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{1}^{e}(\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{t}}+{e}^{2})\\,dt" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186414899}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "316a1f2d-5fe6-5702-992f-674f470a0d8b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{1}^{{e}^{2}}(\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{t}}+{e}^{2}t)\\,dt" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186414845} :answer-field/id #uuid "4c91b1e8-4070-5bcd-8192-983151e5056d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186414907}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "7481ae6d-9732-54c0-9a0f-c47726a9f80a" :question/math-academy-id "q-125143" :question/problem "Which of the following is equivalent to the integral ${\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r,$ where $F(x,y)=\\sqrt{y}\\,i+{e}^{x}\\,j$ and $C$ is the curve $\\operatorname{ln}⁡y=x-2$ traversed from the point $A(2,1)$ to the point $B(4,{e}^{2})?$\n\n*Hint: Use the parametrization $r(t)=(\\operatorname{ln}⁡t+2)\\,i+t\\,j.$*" :question/worked-solution "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r={\\int }_{a}^{b}F(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t)\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nFirst, we need to parameterize the curve. This is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =(\\operatorname{ln}⁡t+2)\\,i+t\\,j,\\,1\\le t\\le {e}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, along the curve $C,$ we have\n\n$$\nx=\\operatorname{ln}⁡t+2,\\,y=t.\n$$\n\nAs a result, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(t)) & =\\sqrt{y}\\,i+{e}^{x}\\,j \\\\  & =\\sqrt{t}\\,i+{e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡t+2}\\,j \\\\  & =\\sqrt{t}\\,i+t{e}^{2}\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing ${r}^{′}(t),$ we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡t+2)i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t)j \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{t}\\,i+j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t) & =(\\sqrt{t}\\,i+t{e}^{2}\\,j)\\cdot (\\frac{1}{t}\\,i+j) \\\\  & =\\sqrt{t}\\cdot \\frac{1}{t}+t{e}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{t}}+{e}^{2}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can write the integral as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r & ={\\int }_{1}^{{e}^{2}}F(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{{e}^{2}}(\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{t}}+{e}^{2}t)\\,dt.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186414918 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179735 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "5d7c30c2-5d42-5648-ae49-5a0c7ec43233" :knowledge-point/title "Line Integrals Over Circles and Ellipses"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "02e28cc7-f7f3-51e1-bea0-158eef714f3a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186414829} {:answer/id #uuid "08339a10-b80f-5555-8e33-e4cd3d9e86b5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{7}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186414831} {:answer/id #uuid "1263d5b8-2a16-5cf4-bfba-339ea29a3a16" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{7}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186414837} {:answer/id #uuid "5773a110-68a4-55f1-abfc-418c6517f35e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{9}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186414851} {:answer/id #uuid "7b6ccc95-f5af-538e-adc2-b6c8fdfcacc8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{9}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186414864}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7b6ccc95-f5af-538e-adc2-b6c8fdfcacc8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{9}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186414864} :answer-field/id #uuid "a60002fd-79c9-55af-8350-d15ad8aa640e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186414911}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "17a6c1da-e7e7-5509-a937-e43376741415" :question/math-academy-id "q-125234" :question/problem "Evaluate ${\\int }_{C}F(r)\\cdot \\text{d}r,$ where $F(x,y)=x\\,i+{y}^{2}\\,j$ and $C$ is the arc of a circle of radius $3$ centered at the origin traversed counterclockwise from the point $(0,3)$ to the point $(-3,0).$" :question/worked-solution "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r={\\int }_{a}^{b}F(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t)\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nFirst, we need to parameterize a part-circle of radius $3$ centered at the origin. This curve can be parameterized as \n\n$$\nr(t)=3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,i+3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,j,\\,\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\le t\\le \\pi .\n$$\n\nSo, along the curve $C,$ we have\n\n$$\nx=3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,y=3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t.\n$$\n\nAs a result,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(t)) & =x\\,i+{y}^{2}\\,j \\\\  & =3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,i+9{sin}^{2}⁡t\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing ${r}^{′}(t),$ we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)j \\\\  & =-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,i+3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t) & =(3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,i+9{sin}^{2}⁡t\\,j)\\cdot (-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,i+3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,j) \\\\  & =-9\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+27{sin}^{2}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\  & =9\\operatorname{cos}⁡t(3{sin}^{2}⁡t-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can evaluate the integral, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}F(r)\\cdot \\text{d}r & ={\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{\\pi }F(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{\\pi }9\\operatorname{cos}⁡t(3{sin}^{2}⁡t-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)\\,dt \\\\  & =9{\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{\\pi }\\operatorname{cos}⁡t(3{sin}^{2}⁡t-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)\\,dt \\\\  & =9{[{sin}^{3}⁡t-\\frac{{sin}^{2}⁡t}{2}]}_{\\pi /2}^{\\pi } \\\\  & =9[0-(1-\\frac{1}{2})] \\\\  & =-\\frac{9}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186414917 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179735 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "5d7c30c2-5d42-5648-ae49-5a0c7ec43233" :knowledge-point/title "Line Integrals Over Circles and Ellipses"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0a2b4e96-c35f-5329-ad67-9ac58f667665" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "32\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186414833} {:answer/id #uuid "5d05fea1-c2dc-5ed1-983c-3cd1bdea51ce" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "16\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186414853} {:answer/id #uuid "63baee51-af86-53ee-921b-9301ac9943be" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "32" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186414856} {:answer/id #uuid "d8247ec2-f7ab-5814-8d82-ad9c6d067e3e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186414894} {:answer/id #uuid "f8b7ad7b-8dd4-56ba-9f6e-701e837c4775" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "16" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186414901}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d8247ec2-f7ab-5814-8d82-ad9c6d067e3e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186414894} :answer-field/id #uuid "70d6c2d2-5cd3-5ed9-a60d-0559257216bb" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186414909}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "019e6ad0-f5c1-533f-a132-64a664a1964d" :question/math-academy-id "q-125235" :question/problem "Evaluate ${∮}_{C}F(r)\\cdot \\text{d}r,$ where $F(x,y)=-x\\,i+y\\,j$ and $C$ is a circle of radius $4$ centered at the origin, traversed once in the counterclockwise direction." :question/worked-solution "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r={\\int }_{a}^{b}F(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t)\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nFirst, we need to parameterize a circle of radius $4$ centered at the origin. It can be parameterized as \n\n$$\nr(t)=4\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,i+4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,j,\\,0\\le t\\le 2\\pi .\n$$\n\nSo, along the curve $C,$ we have\n\n$$\nx=4\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,y=4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t.\n$$\n\nAs a result,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(t)) & =x\\,i+y\\,j \\\\  & =-4\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,i+4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing ${r}^{′}(t),$ we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(-4\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)j \\\\  & =-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,i+4\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t) & =(-4\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,i+4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,j)\\cdot (-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,i+4\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,j) \\\\  & =16\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+16\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\  & =32\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\  & =16\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can evaluate the integral, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}F(r)\\cdot \\text{d}r & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }F(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }16\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t\\,dt \\\\  & =-8\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t){|}_{0}^{2\\pi } \\\\  & =-8+8 \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180172 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179735 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "5d7c30c2-5d42-5648-ae49-5a0c7ec43233" :knowledge-point/title "Line Integrals Over Circles and Ellipses"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "41ffa8da-77e2-5ad2-bae3-6db12b358938" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,{e}^{\\operatorname{cos}⁡t}+\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,{e}^{-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t}\\,dt" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186414848} {:answer/id #uuid "61d9375e-62b8-5dc5-b103-2e95fb008d31" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{e}^{\\operatorname{cos}⁡t}+{e}^{-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t}\\,dt" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186414855} {:answer/id #uuid "89b35800-42e0-534e-9730-7f543a8d03ac" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,dt" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186414874} {:answer/id #uuid "973d4a62-26aa-5079-a393-3d73fa6f1bdc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,{e}^{\\operatorname{cos}⁡t}+\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,{e}^{-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t}\\,dt" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186414878} {:answer/id #uuid "c0a88e5c-5edd-53db-b49c-ff476f854690" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\sqrt{\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t}\\,dt" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186414887}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "973d4a62-26aa-5079-a393-3d73fa6f1bdc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,{e}^{\\operatorname{cos}⁡t}+\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,{e}^{-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t}\\,dt" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186414878} :answer-field/id #uuid "c6e03b21-80ec-5be8-9b81-321307ca2b39" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186414912}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "e19e4fc2-d0da-5e51-a13e-b5b2b6e03a48" :question/math-academy-id "q-39341" :question/problem "![](images/48/4807f92defb4202359a32ca046073cd138db396a0975164e42b96126ac0cc591.png)\n\nWhich of the following is equivalent to ${\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r,$ where $F(x,y)={e}^{x}\\,i+{e}^{-2y}\\,j$ and $C$ is the arc of a circle of radius $1$ centered at the origin traversed counterclockwise from the point $(1,0)$ to the point $(0,1)?$\n\n*Hint: Use the parametrization $r(t)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,i+\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,j.$*" :question/worked-solution "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r={\\int }_{a}^{b}F(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t)\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nFirst, we need to parameterize a part-circle of radius $1$ centered at the origin. This curve can be parameterized as \n\n$$\nr(t)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,i+\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,j,\\,0\\le t\\le \\frac{\\pi }{2}.\n$$\n\nSo, along the curve $C,$ we have\n\n$$\nx=\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,y=\\operatorname{sin}⁡t.\n$$\n\nAs a result,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(t)) & ={e}^{x}\\,i+{e}^{-2y}\\,j \\\\  & ={e}^{\\operatorname{cos}⁡t}\\,i+{e}^{-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t}\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing ${r}^{′}(t),$ we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)j \\\\  & =-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,i+\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t) & =({e}^{\\operatorname{cos}⁡t}\\,i+{e}^{-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t}\\,j)\\cdot (-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,i+\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,j) \\\\  & ={e}^{\\operatorname{cos}⁡t}\\cdot (-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)+{e}^{-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t}\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\  & =-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,{e}^{\\operatorname{cos}⁡t}+\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,{e}^{-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can write the integral as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}F(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,{e}^{\\operatorname{cos}⁡t}+\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,{e}^{-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t}\\,dt.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186414926 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179735 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "5d7c30c2-5d42-5648-ae49-5a0c7ec43233" :knowledge-point/title "Line Integrals Over Circles and Ellipses"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "00790b3e-1877-50c1-bfcc-d335a0c8a0b2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "72{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,dt" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186414827} {:answer/id #uuid "130b870f-02ff-5d3c-ab1b-056597b4e00c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "72{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t{cos}^{2}⁡t\\,dt" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186414838} {:answer/id #uuid "5c129e0b-5a66-500a-8c3c-d6ffe9ba1b8d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+1)\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,dt" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186414852} {:answer/id #uuid "84d43f47-82cb-5b0d-880b-dfce30bd84f1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "27{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{sin}^{2}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,dt" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186414872} {:answer/id #uuid "f736b33d-d8b9-5e81-957d-ff8808e1d1e9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "27{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\sqrt{\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t}\\,dt" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186414900}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "00790b3e-1877-50c1-bfcc-d335a0c8a0b2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "72{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,dt" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186414827} :answer-field/id #uuid "ce1ed3f7-4051-55ee-8dd8-c7e73caeb3e4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186414914}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "d9322f25-3d78-5fb4-87e7-1e944f82fbf4" :question/math-academy-id "q-76984" :question/problem "![](images/fb/fb8c2591ed5068812fa1d184d4a93a6c71b0d2ef77bb01debd4b2a03211742f7.png)\n\nWhich of the following is equivalent to the integral ${\\int }_{C}F(r)\\cdot \\text{d}r,$ where $F(x,y)=2x\\,i+5y\\,j$ and $C$ is the the arc of the ellipse centered at the origin with horizontal and vertical semiaxes $2$ and $4$ respectively, traversed anticlockwise from $A(2,0)$ to $B(0,4).$\n\n*Hint: Use the parametrization $r(t)=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,i+4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,j.$*" :question/worked-solution "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r={\\int }_{a}^{b}F(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t)\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nFirst, we need to find the parametrization of an ellipse centered at the origin with horizontal and vertical semiaxes $2$ and $4$ respectively. It can be parametrized as \n\n$$\nr(t)=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,i+4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,j,\\,0\\le t\\le \\frac{\\pi }{2}.\n$$\n\nSo, along the curve $C,$ we have\n\n$$\nx=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,y=4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t.\n$$\n\nAs a result,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(t)) & =2x\\,i+5y\\,j \\\\  & =4\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,i+20\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing ${r}^{′}(t),$ we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)j \\\\  & =-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,i+4\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t) & =(4\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,i+20\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,j)\\cdot (-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,i+4\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,j) \\\\  & =4\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\cdot (-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)+20\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\cdot 4\\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\  & =72\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can write the integral as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}F(r)\\cdot \\text{d}r & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}F(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =72{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,dt.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}]}
